{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is plausible, but it needs a country, timeframe, and definition of “university leadership” to assess accurately. That could mean university presidents, governing boards, deans, or department heads—and representation can differ across those roles.\n\n“Underrepresented” also needs a benchmark: the Black share of leadership compared with the Black share of the national population, students, academic staff, or people eligible for those positions. Each comparison answers a different question.\n\nA defensible version would be: “In [country/year], Black people held X% of [specified leadership roles], compared with Y% of [specified comparison group].” Without those details and supporting data, the claim is too broad to confirm. A measured disparity would establish underrepresentation relative to that benchmark; it would not, by itself, establish its causes."}}
